#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166569 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166952 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d3cb2fcb-3457-5a89-8968-6c0f2c2cfa83" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Product of Cosines"}] :question/id #uuid "177a0110-424b-5ac3-a24e-e817cbcf479c" :question/math-academy-id "e-21823" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral $\\displaystyle \\int_{\\pi/4}^{\\pi/2} \\cos(9x)\\cos(x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the sum and difference formulas for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta ) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta  \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta ) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these equations, we get the following product-to-sum identity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta )+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta ) & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta  \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta )+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta ))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(9x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x) & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(9x-x)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(9x+x)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8x)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(10x)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the integral gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(9x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8x)+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(10x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{16}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8x)+\\frac{1}{20}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(10x)]}_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{16}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi ))+\\frac{1}{20}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{2})) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{16}(0-0)+\\frac{1}{20}(0-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{20}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166570 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166953 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8a371de4-7c3f-5687-bd3a-d3ab5ab803ce" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Product of Sines"}] :question/id #uuid "ebf57ac7-29e3-595e-8a72-032e10c470aa" :question/math-academy-id "e-21824" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral $\\displaystyle \\int \\sin(12x)\\sin(5x)\\,\\text{d}x .$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the sum and difference formulas for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta ) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta  \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta ) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubtracting the second equation from the first, we get the following product-to-sum identity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta ) & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta ))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(12x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x) & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(12x-5x)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(12x+5x)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7x)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(17x)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the integral gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(12x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7x)-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(17x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{14}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7x)-\\frac{1}{34}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(17x)+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary constant."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166571 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166954 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9d3b7f35-fcd1-5443-b0ea-0b06e016e0c3" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Product of Sine and Cosine"}] :question/id #uuid "14466847-40e1-5a4f-b50a-23b62590f0a2" :question/math-academy-id "e-21825" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral $\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{\\pi/2} \\sin(2x)\\cos(4x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the sum and difference formulas for sine:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta ) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta ) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these equations, we get the following product-to-sum identity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta )+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta ) & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta )+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta ))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x) & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x+4x)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x-4x)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-2x)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the integral gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x)-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{1}{12}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)+\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)]}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{12}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\pi )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0))+\\frac{1}{4}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{12}(-1-1)+\\frac{1}{4}(-1-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167279 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166953 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8a371de4-7c3f-5687-bd3a-d3ab5ab803ce" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Product of Sines"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fb997472-86c5-5cad-89b9-8ea1ca0030a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546814}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fb997472-86c5-5cad-89b9-8ea1ca0030a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546814} :answer-field/id #uuid "7ed943c4-5d0d-5d06-8135-a0d7948bb9ae" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164892}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7721acbc-048b-50e9-95a0-46bf90727bd9" :question/math-academy-id "q-340386" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x)\\,\\text{d}x=$ {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the sum and difference formulas for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta ) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta  \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta ) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubtracting the second equation from the first, we get the following product-to-sum identity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta ) & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta ))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x) & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x-6x)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x+6x)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(9x)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the integral gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(9x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)-\\frac{1}{18}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(9x)]}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0))-\\frac{1}{18}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{9\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}(-1-0)-\\frac{1}{18}(1-0) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167281 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166954 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9d3b7f35-fcd1-5443-b0ea-0b06e016e0c3" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Product of Sine and Cosine"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "825d9664-8352-5088-b23a-b2777e89df08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546540}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "825d9664-8352-5088-b23a-b2777e89df08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546540} :answer-field/id #uuid "ad646b86-c5af-5b0e-9b9f-e5764cd74edd" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164899}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3d707fb1-cb96-50fe-b7df-5abab3385ab6" :question/math-academy-id "q-340390" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)\\,\\text{d}x=$ {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the sum and difference formulas for sine:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta ) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta ) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these equations, we get the following product-to-sum identity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta )+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta ) & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta )+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta ))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x) & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x+6x)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x-6x)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(9x)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-3x)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(9x)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the integral gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(9x)-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{1}{18}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(9x)+\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)]}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{18}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{9\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0))+\\frac{1}{6}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{18}(0-1)+\\frac{1}{6}(0-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314946 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166952 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d3cb2fcb-3457-5a89-8968-6c0f2c2cfa83" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Product of Cosines"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7511559d-1260-514d-8be4-3e7cc538c779" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{12}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x)+\\frac{1}{32}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(16x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312908} {:answer/id #uuid "9f1be971-8240-5314-bbe0-c73114377645" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x)+\\frac{1}{16}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(16x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313227} {:answer/id #uuid "c3edf8c4-f1ba-521f-a323-8a7f41301969" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x)+\\frac{1}{11}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(11x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313513} {:answer/id #uuid "d7c77b1d-3b97-5c81-b48d-a10543aa60d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{12}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)-\\frac{1}{32}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(16x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313675} {:answer/id #uuid "e72f93f6-ae01-5923-a6b6-ede474ef1bb9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{12}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x)-\\frac{1}{32}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(16x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313808}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7511559d-1260-514d-8be4-3e7cc538c779" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{12}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x)+\\frac{1}{32}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(16x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312908} :answer-field/id #uuid "fe0bde4e-5f8d-5d47-a561-5a92587b89b4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314692}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b947f05f-96e9-547a-a382-8c4c319c6eca" :question/math-academy-id "q-340455" :question/problem "$\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡(11x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the sum and difference formulas for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta ) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta  \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta ) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these equations, we get the following product-to-sum identity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta )+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta ) & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta  \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta )+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta ))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(11x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x) & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(11x-5x)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(11x+5x)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(16x)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the integral gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡(11x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(16x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x)+\\frac{1}{32}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(16x)+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary constant."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186546996 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166952 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d3cb2fcb-3457-5a89-8968-6c0f2c2cfa83" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Product of Cosines"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bb26691a-385a-57d1-8078-1f35f18c73ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{21}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546669}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bb26691a-385a-57d1-8078-1f35f18c73ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{21}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546669} :answer-field/id #uuid "767e5e84-d488-5271-8138-b5fbcff6b6b6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546879}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "421776ee-f665-581f-82b5-c07ca0624bc4" :question/math-academy-id "q-340458" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the sum and difference formulas for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta ) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta  \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta ) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these equations, we get the following product-to-sum identity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta )+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta ) & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta  \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta )+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta ))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x) & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x-5x)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x+5x)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-3x)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7x)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7x)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the integral gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+\\frac{1}{14}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7x)]}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0))+\\frac{1}{14}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}(-1-0)+\\frac{1}{14}(-1-0) \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{21}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547064 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166952 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d3cb2fcb-3457-5a89-8968-6c0f2c2cfa83" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Product of Cosines"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3a0b7907-741b-5293-b278-a9e4a5f33e9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546375}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3a0b7907-741b-5293-b278-a9e4a5f33e9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546375} :answer-field/id #uuid "b96d03be-bed3-558c-a1ef-99668bcb5dc0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546923}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dc83095a-e05b-5bdd-9ae4-88a129415b52" :question/math-academy-id "q-340459" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x=$ {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the sum and difference formulas for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta ) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta  \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta ) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these equations, we get the following product-to-sum identity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta )+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta ) & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta  \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta )+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta ))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x) & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x-x)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x+x)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the integral gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{8}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)+\\frac{1}{12}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x)]}_{0}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0))+\\frac{1}{12}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}(0-0)+\\frac{1}{12}(-1-0) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547060 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166952 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d3cb2fcb-3457-5a89-8968-6c0f2c2cfa83" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Product of Cosines"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "32660273-053d-57c2-8679-1174253ff668" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546360}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "32660273-053d-57c2-8679-1174253ff668" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546360} :answer-field/id #uuid "691cc2fe-d17d-54b1-9079-15c5e5316c91" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546871}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d4d3424f-8de3-594c-bbdf-8f7aff226934" :question/math-academy-id "q-340461" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral.\n\n${\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x=$ {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the sum and difference formulas for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta ) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta  \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta ) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these equations, we get the following product-to-sum identity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta )+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta ) & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta  \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta )+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta ))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x) & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x-3x)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x+3x)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8x)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the integral gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi }\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+\\frac{1}{16}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8x)]}_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}))+\\frac{1}{16}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi )) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(0-1)+\\frac{1}{16}(0-0) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547007 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166952 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d3cb2fcb-3457-5a89-8968-6c0f2c2cfa83" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Product of Cosines"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ea6691d6-cc63-5f49-a64d-f75321108ff4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546779}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ea6691d6-cc63-5f49-a64d-f75321108ff4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546779} :answer-field/id #uuid "f3f3cb93-d5b9-5bb2-99da-ae2d6fb22250" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-fie